      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bessemer City takes its name from Sir Henry Bessemer, the inventor of the iron‑smelting Bessemer process.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessemer_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Old Ormand Furnace, erected in the 1760s, remains one of the nation’s oldest iron furnaces and a historic monument.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://northcarolinahistory.org/encyclopedia/bessemer-city/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1891, John Askew Smith built the first luxurious home, Long View, and lived there for nearly twenty years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessemer_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When the Bessemer process made small furnaces uneconomical, Bessemer City’s iron industry faded, giving way to railroads and agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessemer_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Perched at 904 feet, Bessemer City lies roughly 10 miles northwest of Gastonia and 40 miles west of Charlotte.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
